-- title:
--   Fibre dimension of a finite power-series quotient under specialisation
-- statement:
--   Let $B$ be a commutative Noetherian ring, let $n$ be a natural number, and let $\rho : \mathrm{Fin}\,n \to B[\![x_1,\dots,x_n]\!]$ be a family of $n$ multivariate power series in $n$ variables over $B$ such that each $\rho_i$ has zero constant coefficient. Assume there is an $N$ with $x_i^N$ in the ideal generated by the range of $\rho$ for every $i$, and that the quotient $B[\![x]\!]/(\rho_1,\dots,\rho_n)$ is a finite $B$-module. Let $\kappa$ and $\kappa'$ be fields and $f : B \to \kappa$, $g : B \to \kappa'$ ring homomorphisms with $\ker g \subseteq \ker f$. Then the $\kappa$-dimension of $\kappa[\![x]\!]$ modulo the ideal spanned by the coefficientwise images $\mathrm{map}\,f\,(\rho_i)$ equals the $\kappa'$-dimension of $\kappa'[\![x]\!]$ modulo the ideal spanned by the images $\mathrm{map}\,g\,(\rho_i)$. Both dimensions are `Module.finrank`, so the assertion is an equality of natural numbers; the finiteness hypothesis over $B$ is what makes them the genuine fibre dimensions.
--
--   This is the statement that the fibre dimension of a finite infinitesimal power-series quotient is constant along a specialisation of points: the point of $f$ lies in the closure of the point of $g$ exactly when $\ker g \subseteq \ker f$. It is used in the study of formal $\mathcal{O}_D$-modules, in the recognition of isogenies of height four and in the construction of Cartier quadruples attached to edge isogenies.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_MvPowerSeries_finrank_quotient_map_eq_of_ker_le.lean

